          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines maybe six times a month.",
          "answer": "Six migraine attacks a month is a pattern worth discussing with a clinician. This tool can't tell you whether it is migraine or what to do about it.\n\nThe main point from the migraine guidance is about how often you take acute medicine for an attack. Frequent use can keep headaches going. The guidance gives these limits: fewer than 10 treatment days a month for triptans, ergots, opioids and combination pain relievers, and fewer than 15 days a month for simple pain relievers. Counting treatment days, not attacks, is the useful check. Overuse is common and reversible, and clinicians check for it before changing a preventive plan (National headache society guidance, migraine guideline).\n\nThe condition reference describes migraine as episodic for most people. Chronic migraine is defined as headache on 15 or more days a month for over three months (General clinical reference, Migraine). Six attacks a month does not meet that definition on its face, but only a clinician can judge it.\n\nGet urgent care right away if a severe headache comes on within about a minute and reaches full strength, or if a headache comes with fever and a stiff neck, new numbness, weakness or other neurological change, or follows a head injury (National headache society guidance, migraine guideline).\n\nThe sources I searched do not cover preventive treatment options, doses, or specific medicines, so I can't say what would suit you. A useful next step is to bring a diary of attacks and how often you take medicine for them to a doctor or pharmacist.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
