          "session_id": "full-mt-m-003",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is that enough to need something preventive?",
          "answer": "The migraine guidance gives a rough threshold for when preventive treatment is usually offered. It says preventive treatment is offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not enough. As a guide, it cites four or more migraine days a month with some disability. It also says fewer attacks can qualify if the attacks are severely disabling, if acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or if the person prefers prevention (National headache society guidance, migraine guideline).\n\nSix attacks a month is above that four-day guide, so it may be worth raising with a clinician. Whether it needs prevention depends on how much the attacks disrupt your life and how well your current treatment works. Your clinician will weigh that, not this tool.\n\nThe guidance also says preventive treatment aims to reduce how often and how severely attacks happen, not to end them all. A 50 percent cut in migraine days is the usual measure of a good response.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not describe which preventive options exist, their doses, or which might suit you, so I can't say more about that. Bringing your attack diary, including how many days you disrupted and how many days you took medicine, will help that conversation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
